INSTRUCTION FORMATS AND DATA PAYLOADS.

Scope: portable standard DEX. The following twenty-six formats are used
by standard instructions. Unused opcode slots have nominal format 10x in
the opcode tables; that does not authorize execution. 00x is a pseudo-format
for unused values in the separate general format reference, not a length.

Letters name operand fields, not a compulsory grammar for English operations.
Every displayed group is one sixteen-bit code unit. In B:A:op the high nibble
is B, the next nibble is A, and the low byte is op. An AA field occupies an
entire high byte. ØØ means eight zero bits. A field mentioned as signed is
sign-extended from its encoded width. Register and reference indices and
argument counts are unsigned. A wide register operand names its first word.

10x, one unit: ØØ:op. No operands.
12x, one unit: B:A:op. A and B are four-bit register indices.
11n, one unit: B:A:op. A is a four-bit register; B is a signed four-bit literal.
11x, one unit: AA:op. A is an eight-bit register index.
10t, one unit: AA:op. A is a signed eight-bit branch displacement.
20t, two units: ØØ:op AAAA. A is a signed sixteen-bit branch displacement.
22x, two units: AA:op BBBB. A is an eight-bit register; B is sixteen-bit.
21t, two units: AA:op BBBB. A is an eight-bit register; B is a signed
sixteen-bit branch displacement.
21s, two units: AA:op BBBB. A is an eight-bit register; B is a signed
sixteen-bit literal extended to the destination value width.
21h, two units: AA:op BBBB. A is an eight-bit register or pair start.
For const/high16 place B in result bits 31 through 16 and zero bits 15
through 0. For const-wide/high16 place B in bits 63 through 48 and zero
bits 47 through 0. Neither interpretation fills the low bits with sign bits.
21c, two units: AA:op BBBB. A is an eight-bit register or pair start;
B is a sixteen-bit index in the table identified by this opcode.
23x, two units: AA:op CC:BB. A, B and C are eight-bit register indices.
22b, two units: AA:op CC:BB. A and B are eight-bit registers;
C is a signed eight-bit literal extended to thirty-two bits.
22t, two units: B:A:op CCCC. A and B are four-bit registers;
C is a signed sixteen-bit branch displacement.
22s, two units: B:A:op CCCC. A and B are four-bit registers;
C is a signed sixteen-bit literal extended to thirty-two bits.
22c, two units: B:A:op CCCC. A and B are four-bit registers or pair starts;
C is a sixteen-bit index in the opcode's type or field table.
30t, three units: ØØ:op AAAA-low AAAA-high. A is a signed thirty-two-bit
branch displacement assembled from the following two code units.
32x, three units: ØØ:op AAAA BBBB. A and B are sixteen-bit register indices.
31i, three units: AA:op BBBB-low BBBB-high. A is an eight-bit register or
pair start. B is thirty-two bits, sign-extended for const-wide/32 and copied
without numerical conversion for const.
31t, three units: AA:op BBBB-low BBBB-high. A is an eight-bit register.
B is a signed thirty-two-bit displacement to the matching data payload.
31c, three units: AA:op BBBB-low BBBB-high. A is an eight-bit register;
B is an unsigned thirty-two-bit string index.
35c, three units: A:G:op BBBB F:E:D:C. A is a four-bit WORD count,
restricted to zero through five. Select C, then D, E, F, G, stopping after
A words. Every selected register has a four-bit index. B is a sixteen-bit
type, method or call-site index, as specified by the opcode. Unselected
register fields do not contribute arguments. They do not permit a sixth word.
3rc, three units: AA:op BBBB CCCC. A is an eight-bit WORD count;
B is a sixteen-bit index; C is the sixteen-bit first register. Select exactly
A consecutive words starting at C. The interval must fit in the frame.
45cc, four units: A:G:op BBBB F:E:D:C HHHH. The list and count are as
in 35c; B is a sixteen-bit method index and H a sixteen-bit prototype index.
4rcc, four units: AA:op BBBB CCCC HHHH. The interval is as in 3rc;
B is a sixteen-bit method index and H a sixteen-bit prototype index.
51l, five units: AA:op BBBB-low BBBB-next BBBB-next BBBB-high.
A is an eight-bit pair start; B is the entire sixty-four-bit constant.

PACKED SWITCH PAYLOAD, IDENTIFIER 0x0100.

At an even code-unit offset, store identifier 0x0100, an unsigned
sixteen-bit entry count, a signed thirty-two-bit first key, then that many
signed thirty-two-bit target displacements. The length is four plus twice
the entry count, in code units. Key number j is first_key plus j. The key
sequence must remain in the signed thirty-two-bit range; wrapping the last
key to a negative integer is not a permitted key sequence. A match uses
the corresponding displacement added to the SWITCH instruction's index.
If the tested signed integer lies outside that consecutive key interval,
the switch falls through. Zero entries give no match.

SPARSE SWITCH PAYLOAD, IDENTIFIER 0x0200.

Store identifier 0x0200, an unsigned sixteen-bit entry count, that many
signed thirty-two-bit keys, and then that many signed thirty-two-bit target
displacements. The keys must be strictly increasing, without duplicates.
This is two separate arrays, not an interleaving of each key with its target.
The length is two plus four times the entry count, in code units. A matching
key uses the target at the same array index, relative to the switch
instruction. A missing key, including in an empty table, falls through.

ARRAY DATA PAYLOAD, IDENTIFIER 0x0300.

Store identifier 0x0300, an unsigned sixteen-bit element width in BYTES,
an unsigned thirty-two-bit element count, then the element bytes in order.
Portable primitive element widths are one, two, four or eight bytes.
The width must agree with the actual primitive array component type.
No object references may be embedded by this mechanism. The total length
is four plus the ceiling of width times count divided by two, in CODE UNITS.
If the byte count is odd, the final byte merely pads the final code unit;
it does not supply an extra array element. A fill copies exactly the stated
elements to array indices zero through count minus one. An array may be
larger; the remaining elements are unchanged. All size arithmetic used to
validate the file must reject overflow rather than wrap an extent.

The payload alignment, identifier, extent and branch-target checks belong
to decoding/verification. Nullness and an actual array shorter than the
payload count are runtime conditions. Payload data has no sequential
executable meaning and never supplies an implicit return instruction.

NONPORTABLE FORMATS.

20bc, 22cs, 35ms, 35mi, 3rms and 3rmi describe suggested or historical
linked/optimized formats. Their appearance in the format reference does
not allocate a standard portable opcode. See "Optimized instructions are
a different namespace.english" and the separate foreign decoder snapshot.

SOURCE REFERENCES.

